1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:12,316
I am the centre millwright. I work for the centre's administrator, the registrar, and keep each project's access the same everywhere: its Slack channel, its code and its data folders.

2
00:00:12,700 --> 00:00:27,081
Each project has a list of its members. I compare that list with who is actually in the project's channel, on its code, and on its folders on the shared servers. Any difference is drift, and I report each one with what I would change.

3
00:00:27,081 --> 00:00:39,441
I also set up projects whose members come from several labs, inviting the others as guests. I work one project at a time, and change nothing until the registrar approves.

4
00:00:39,850 --> 00:00:49,773
Each lab's own millwright sets up that lab's members; I take over once a project crosses labs. I read each lab's records, which the lab's PI keeps.

5
00:00:49,773 --> 00:00:56,653
When a channel is no longer needed, I archive it, so the registrar can bring it back.

6
00:00:57,050 --> 00:01:03,119
A real run that changes nothing. The registrar asks me to check the centre's records on this machine.

7
00:01:03,119 --> 00:01:13,081
The comparison of members with their access could not run here, and I say so. I then list eight local items, each with what I would do.

8
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:23,748
To use me, ask the centre millwright, on the registrar's computer, to check one project or every project. I show each difference before anything changes.

9
00:01:23,748 --> 00:01:40,862
The first line is the verdict: Reconciled, Drift, or Blocked while I wait for approval. A centre sets its own name, Slack workspace and GitHub organisation, and every project's names follow from them.
